  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/horadandia-brittani-a-dwarf-from-india-2/",
      "html": "<p>Horadandia brittani, the dwarf fire rasbora, is a miniature fish reaching only 1.5–2 cm in length. It is robust and easy to care for, and is ideal for nano aquariums.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/horadandia-brittani-a-dwarf-from-india-2/",
      "html": "<p>This fish comes from South India (Kerala). It is closely related to the fire rasboras of the genus Rasboroides.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/horadandia-brittani-a-dwarf-from-india-2/",
      "html": "<p>The water values (pH and hardness) are insignificant for care; any drinkable water is suitable. The water temperature can be between 22 and 28°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/horadandia-brittani-a-dwarf-from-india-2/",
      "html": "<p>They should be kept in shoals.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/horadandia-brittani-a-dwarf-from-india-2/",
      "html": "<p>For a long time it was believed that there was only one species, Horadandia atukorali. It was discovered in 1943 in Sri Lanka, and later Horadandia was also found in South India. The South Indian population was described as the subspecies H. a. brittani in 1992, but this subspecies was initially not accepted by the scientific community and was declared synonymous. Only in 2013 did another revision of the genus show that brittani was not only valid but even an independent species. No differences can be recognised with the naked eye, so in the trade usually no distinction is made between H. atukorali and H. brittani.</p>"
    }
  ],
